WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.260
So, back in February

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.780
I received the following letter from a student at Linfield College

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.780
And, according to its website,

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.860
"With campuses in McMinnville, Portland, and a prestigious online program

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:24.240
Linfield College is a premier choice in the Pacific Northwest for a high quality, traditional liberal arts education

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.520
Want to learn more?

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.840
Contact us for details about our degree and certificate program offerings."

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.080
A student there, who I won't name, sent me the following letter:

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.800
"Dear Dr. Peterson,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.920
I'm an undergraduate student at Linfield College at McMinnville, Oregon,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:50.040
The International Students for Liberty Conference recently granted me a small sum to organize pro-liberty events on campus,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.180
with an emphasis on the importance of free speech

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.220
I'm writing to ask you if you're accepting speaking invitations at this time,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:03.340
and, if you're scheduled sometime between now and May would allow it to help narrow down my options.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:09.580
I would like to offer you 1500 dollars and an invitation to speak here this spring semester.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.460
Your travel and accommodation costs will also be covered.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.300
I perceive Linfield as an institution at an ideological crossroads.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:25.300
I feel it is my responsibility to pick the most effective voice possible for helping Linfield chart a course

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.220
that will insure its future as a place of reason and academic integrity.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.680
After listening to many of your lectures and interviews, I believe your message is ideal for our campus.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:40.140
Honest and productive argumentation is one of your specialties.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.340
Linfield would greatly benefit from being introduced to your ideas on the subject.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.680
We are a small college of roughly 2000 students, situated in quiet wine country.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.440
Your work continues to have a deep impact on my personal and intellectual growth.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.620
I'd be honored to hear from you.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.280
Thank you for your time and consideration."

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.520
So, after considering it for a while,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.920
I agreed to come speak at Linfield College.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:13.240
On March 18th, I received a note from an inviting student, who said:

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.440
"Good morning Dr. Peterson, I would be overjoyed to confirm a date."

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.940
So we went about doing that.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.260
On April 7th, he replied to me again to update me. He said:

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:31.540
"The talk will be held in ICE auditorium, in Melrose Hall, April 24th, beginning at 7 pm.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:39.220
I'm referring to the event simply as 'A Talk with Jordan Peterson' because his speaking style allows for a lot of fluidity in topic.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:45.700
However, the core theme to the event is the importance of Freedom of speech, especially in higher education

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:57.620
Some of the administration/student government have begun to fret about the potential for people to feel 'offended and oppressed' by Peterson's talk.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.000
It's more pushback than I'd expected at Linfield, but I don't think it's a cause for concern.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.800
I find it hard to keep their worries at bay.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.720
And there's a very supportive group of students and community members who have my back.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.560
Free speech will win."

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.620
On April 17th, the following article appeared in student newspaper

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.180
By Kaelia Neal, Editor in Chief, it's called "Hate Symbol on ball riles campus"

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:35.620
The Linfield Young Americans for liberty groups free speech ball outside Walker's Hall on April 12th received attention from students and faculties

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.800
when a student drew Pepe the Frog,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.020
which has recently been appropriated by White supremacist groups.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:49.220
Linfield College posted a picture of the ball on its instagram account, but it was later removed because of the frog's association with alt-right groups

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.700
"As soon as it was pointed out that the photo included the image, the Instagram post was removed."

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.400
President Thomas Hellie said

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:07.160
The free speech ball event, the first event in the Speak Freely series at Linfield encouraged students to express themselves by writing and drawing on the ball to promote free speech.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.740
Pepe the frog is a popular Internet meme that has often appeared in humorous ways.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:18.720
The anti defamation league has listed Pepe the frog on its hate symbols database

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:25.900
"I only heard about it second hand, and I understand that it is a place for free speech, but in all reality it was certainly down a very poor taste."

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.640
Junior John Christianson said.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:39.380
According to its website, the YAL's mission is to "identify, educate, train and mobilize youth activists committed to 'winning on principle.'

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:46.700
Our goal is to cast the leaders of tomorrow and reclaim the policies, candidates, and direction of our government."

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.680
A member of YAL drew the Pepe the frog on the ball, but asked to remain unidentified.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:05:00.720
"I knew the person who drew Pepe, he didn't draw it to be negative, he drew it to be funny."

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.900
Senior Parker Wells, a member of YAL said.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:11.460
He continued, "We understand some groups appropriated the image, but any image could be appropriated."

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:18.900
Wells said his goals with the free speech ball were to get people thinking about the rights to free expression, and to bring more attention to the YAL group.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:27.740
He said: "I understand that it's necessary to set the limits, but I don't think this cartoon frog is a limit to set for free speech.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.500
I find it unfortunate that people can't enjoy this event because of the frog image.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:40.940
As a part of its Speak Freely series, YAL is bringing Dr. Jordan Peterson to campus on Monday, April 24th.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.680
The group is also showing the documentary 'the Red Pill' on Tuesday, May 2nd."

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.400
Professor of English Reshmi Dutt-Ballerstadt said:

00:05:52.140 --> 00:06:01.360
"I find both the invitation of Jordan Peterson and the screening of documentary 'the Red Pill' by Young Americans for Liberty club extremely problematic,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.860
Problematic because neither Peterson nor the film will be promoting dialogues about gendered inclusions,

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.820
but rather be promoting a dangerous and offensive logic of gendered exclusions.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.040
Dutt-Ballerstadt continued:

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:26.740
"The promotion of such exclusionary practices greatly threatens safe spaces for our student, staff, and faculty who belong to marginalized groups and violates our ethos of upholding mutual respect on our campus."

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:36.260
The President's Diversity Advisory Committee will hold a discussion at 5:30 pm on Monday in the pioneer reading room titled

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.180
'Free speech, what it is and what it is not.'

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:43.840
The discussion will examine free speech and members of the Linfield community are encouraged to attend."

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.560
So that's the end of that newspaper article.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.380
So I read that it was forwarded to me,

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.300
soon after, I tweeted the following message:

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.820
"I'm violating some more safe spaces soon

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.820
Linfield College, April 24."

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:10.500
Well, on April 19th, I received a letter from the student who organized my talk,

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.240
he said: "Things have gone nuclear,

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:19.060
this email, the one I'm going to read to you, was just sent to all of Linfield.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.860
One of the stipulations they're referring to is the contract, which they rejected because it was a day late.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.760
"Our club is currently looking for space in downtown McMinnville  to hold Peterson's talk instead.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.720
The media is likely getting involved.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.300
I'm sorry for this insanity, we tried very hard to keep things civil here."

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.480
So here is the letter sent out to the entire community:

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.280
"Linfield Community:

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:53.280
I am writing on behalf of the Linfield cabinet, as officer in charge, while President Kellie is out,

00:07:53.420 --> 00:08:04.300
I want to let the Linfield community know that the ASLC, Associated Students of Linfield College, has determined it will not fund Dr. Jordan Peterson's appearance next week

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:08.580
because stipulations of the event were not met by the requester, Young Americans for Liberty.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:14.820
Since the authorization parameters were not satisfied, the college will not host the event on April 24th.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:19.760
Among the stipulations was that the event was to be a private Linfield College-only event.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.000
By his own tweet yesterday, to 107000 followers,

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:32.220
Dr. Peterson indicates he would be "violating more safe spaces soon, Linfield College"

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:38.200
Like-minded supporters from outside our community have responded affirmatively.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:51.300
Our policy on anti-harassment protection and academic freedom states that "anti-harassment policies are not intended to limit the free exchange of opinions

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.200
or the vigorous debate over ideas."

00:08:53.780 --> 00:09:01.480
However, intimidation, harassment, exploitation, and the use or threat of force are incompatible with the preservation of this freedom.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:04.760
The letter continues:

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:11.080
"Based on the speaker's own words, the event no longer presents the possibility of academic inquiry within the Linfield community.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.520
Our policy is clear:

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:21.440
'Harassment and intimidation preclude the very possibility for maintaining atmosphere of academic freedom.'

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:31.240
We cannot welcome speakers when the stipulations for their appearances are not met and who intend to violate the safety of our community.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.680
Respectfully, Susan Agre-Kippenhan"

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.420
So I wrote a letter to Susan Agre-Kippenhan.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:48.660
Now, you understand that by this time, the flights have been arranged, the accommodations have been arranged, everything was in place.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.660
And of course they've known about this for a long time.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:52.540
"Dear Susan,

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.840
I received a copy of your letter to the Linfield community this morning,

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.040
as well as an indication that my long scheduled talk at Linfield has been canceled,

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.500
that is fine, I suppose,

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:10.860
except that the plane tickets have been booked and the hotel reservations have been made, as well as three days taken out of my schedule at my expense.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:19.300
I think you are treating me and the student group who invited me in a manner that is absolutely reprehensible,

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.760
as well as cowardly and underhanded.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:28.340
I have made arrangements to come to Linfield, which is extremely distant from me, and out of the way, in good faith.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.520
Now you cancel it,

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:36.020
with a plethora of specious reasons including your anti-harassment policy,

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:40.060
although I have harassed no one, nor intimidated, exploited or threatened force.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:47.240
Had I not previously received notice of the controversy of my arrival, which is in my opinion unwarranted,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.960
I would not have tweeted my intent to 'violate safe spaces'

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:56.940
which is a satirical comment in case it needs to be said.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.780
Obviously, you were looking for any excuse, no matter how trivial, to cancel this event.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:10.760
You also note that 'This event no longer presents the possibility of academic inquiry within the Linfield community'

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:18.760
I might point out that Linfield is a college, not a community, and that ideas need to be discussed in a college.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:28.280
You also state that 'Anti-harassment policies are not intended to limit the free exchange of opinions, or the vigorous debate of ideas'

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:31.740
and then precede to do exactly that.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:40.400
You also mentioned that you have received support from 'like-minded supporters' from outside your community,

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.620
which is hardly surprising or informative given that they were like minded.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:52.260
You also suggest that because the contract was delayed one day, that constitutes sufficient grounds for cancellation,

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:59.380
even though you knew perfectly well that the event was booked and finalized, and that such delays are by no means unheard of in such situations.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:07.380
You might also note that a guest invited to your college might expect to be treated with a modicum of consideration and respect.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:09.440
Here's what I plan to do:

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:19.000
I am going to make a video tomorrow morning at the latest, reading this letter on Youtube, explaining that my invitation has been revoked, what that means for me,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.000
and the nature of the reasons given

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:25.040
I will also read this letter of response from me.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:33.400
It should be watched by about a 150000 viewers, many of whom I imagine will be Linfield alumni supporters and students,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.840
I am perfectly willing to see what they all have to say.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.120
Then we can let the dust settle where it may.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:45.000
Sincerely, Dr Jordan Peterson, Professor of Psychology, University of Toronto."

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.960
I received absolutely nothing in response.

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:55.360
Got no, uh, they didn't even have the decency to send me a notification explaining their rationale, nothing.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:03.200
So I was treated as far as I'm concerned with, well let's call it unprofessionally and leave it at that.

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:04.740
So what's the story now?

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:09.280
The organizing students tried to book an alternative venue,

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:13.540
they didn't have enough money to rent a suitable theater or lecture hall,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.820
I told them they could use my honorarium to pay for the space,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:19.000
which they did,

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:23.960
The yamhillvalleynewsregister.com posted this update April 20th:

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.960
"Off-campus venue found for controversial speaker"

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:36.140
Linfield College's student government organization this week withdrew permission for a newly organized club,

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:41.600
Young Americans for Liberty, to bring a controversial speaker to campus for a public talk Monday.

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:48.040
The group has responded by lining up an off-campus location for the presentation,

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:49.580
featuring Jordan Peterson,

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:58.660
a Canadian professor whose campaign against gender-neutral pronouns is considered offensive by some to the transgender population.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:03.660
Peterson has been re-booked for 7 to 9 pm at the Falls Event Center,

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:08.760
housed in the former chapel on the Evergreen Aviation &amp; Space Museum campus.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:10.600
So,

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:12.780
on with the show.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:19.200
And you can figure out what I think of the Linfield administrators who pulled the stunt on your own.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:21.100
Thanks for watching.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:27.580
Greeting, members of the Linfield community,

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:32.380
the Linfield community, Young Americans for Liberty,

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.100
and other organizations that supported this event,

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:39.380
and those who traveled long to get here, along with the people on the live stream.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:46.880
My name is Lucas Carter, and I'm the president of the Linfield Chapter of Young Americans for Liberty.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:53.260
*applause*

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:59.080
I'm joined here today by my vice president Keeper Smith,

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:03.460
*applause*

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.000
event coordinator Parker Wells,

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:12.200
*applause*

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:16.220
and secretary Austin Ramsey who is handling tonight's live stream,

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.840
*applause*

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:25.560
So we're the team that organized this and fought hard to ensure that liberty won tonight.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:27.780
Judging by this crowd,

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:29.640
I think we've accomplished just that

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:34.620
But first just a couple of technical things to get out of the way,

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:38.160
we are doing the livestream because we received support from all over the nation,

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:39.880
and I mean all over.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:48.740
So we're asking to stay off the Wi-Fi please, so all our friends and families can enjoy this livestream without any hiccups.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:57.060
Secondly, we do have a donation bucket in the back to help cover Jordan Peterson's travel expenses,

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.240
Right now we're going for 750 dollars,

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.080
and any excess fund will go toward his research.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:07.620
So moving on,

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.240
I'd to talk a little about the club

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:14.080
When Keeper and I started this club at the end of February,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.100
we didn't know what to expect.

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:17.960
We did have a vision, however

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:20.360
given recent political events,

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:27.060
not just the Linfield club, college campuses across America have been slits, volatile.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:31.180
Many people are afraid to speak out over fears,

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:33.040
of fast criticism.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:37.340
Our goal as a club is not to indoctrinate libertarian values,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:46.480
but to engage the public in the community in like open, free debate for the sake of intellectual advancements,

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:51.480
being able to debate, being able to reach conclusions with sanity and

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:53.720
respect to one another's.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:58.100
I'd like to thank leadership, that I just introduced

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:03.700
for all the hard work towards accomplishing this goal that we truly believe in.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:10.340
And finally, I wish the best to next semester's chapter president, Keeper Smith,

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:14.740
who will take over next semester after I graduate.

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:21.860
And more importantly, there is something I need to address to the Linfield community,

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:23.280
on behalf of the club,

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:26.840
*ringtone*

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:37.800
On behalf of the club in the Leadership, I'd like to take this time to publicly apologize for any unintended harm that has occurred over these past two weeks.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:45.960
It is not within the club's intentions to generate any harm, and/or create an environment where people feel unsafe.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.160
I just want to make that clear.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:51.140
We want people to be more open, that's our intention,

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:56.840
and I'm also going to ask the public to not raise any pitchforks at my school,

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.100
I love my school.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:04.520
I really do, I didn't move all the way out here from Florida just to like, trash anything, I love my school.

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:12.020
And beyond that, the situation behind the scenes is a lot more complex than what's been released in public.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:14.640
This club itself has made mistakes too.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:18.820
I just got to say, thank you for cooperation on these matters.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:23.600
Now, I'd like to introduce the main coordinator of this event.

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:26.860
He has done far more work than any of us.

00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:34.880
You know, he's spoke with Peterson. Foreign events, he's spoke with the media, he found his venue last minute, such a beautiful venue, by the way,

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:38.680
He handles most the paperwork, and more,

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:42.760
I can't think of enough. Please welcome, event coordinator, Parker Wells.

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:51.620
*applause*

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:56.260
Hello, everybody, my name is Parker Wells

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:01.480
I'm a senior at Linfield College, and I'm overjoyed to see the response

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:06.620
considering this event had some major complications just last week,

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:11.660
it's overwhelming that the response has been so positive here, so thank you for that.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:17.580
Um, I have to express my gratitude to students of the Liberty and Young Americans for Liberty,

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:25.620
for students who are unsure about how to get involved on your college campuses, I highly recommend the network of liberty minded organizations

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:29.440
because they've been very supportive throughout this whole ordeal, so thank you to them.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:41.140
Lastly, I'd like to frame tonight's talk as an opportunity for us to open our minds and make an honest effort at understanding each other.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:48.000
And on that note, all the way from the University of Toronto, please welcome Dr Jordan Peterson.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:55.020
*Applause intensifies*

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:15.080
Thank you Parker, and also thank you for your persistence.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:23.120
The first thing I'd like to say, is that...

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:31.580
Parker told me ten(10) minutes before the talk that he has very much enjoyed going to Linfield College

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:38.160
which I just had an opportunity to stroll through : it's absolutely spectacular and beautiful.

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:43.780
It looks like everyone's dream of a classic liberal arts college.

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:51.760
So, he wanted me to let everyone know he's had a great time and that he didn't want to bring any disrepute on the college

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:55.680
So, I thought I'd do that because, it's important to know that.

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:01.220
It's easy for things to be made black-and-white, because that's simple.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:06.160
Things aren't a least bit simple. It's useful to keep that in mind.

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:15.040
So, I'm going to start this by reading you something that I wrote specifically for this talk.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:21.260
I wanted / I always want to do something different in each talk because

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:28.180
-well pretty done in the previous talk- and partly the reason that you talk

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:34.820
is to get things clear in your mind. It's a good way of thinking. It's the best way of thinking.

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:40.340
So, I suppose in some sense, it's a / don't know if it's a poem.

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:42.200
Maybe that's what it is.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:47.460
It's what I hope to cover tonight, so what I'm gonna do is read you the outline first. I guess it's an outline.

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:55.320
and then i'm gonna go through it, line by line, and see if I can clarify to you and also to me what I mean

00:21:57.260 --> 00:22:02.400
So, I'm going to orient the talk tonight around the ethics of free speech.

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:08.680
We / you've talked a lot about or caught a lot about , let's say, the right to free speech.

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:14.980
And something you might want also consider the obligation to free speech.

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.840
Because I actually think it's deeper than a right.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:21.040
I think you're obligated to speak freely.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:26.900
Because otherwise, people don't have the benefit or your / whatever wisdom you possess.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.960
And, at least you possess the wisdom of your own experience.

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.180
At the very least, you have that.

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:37.340
And that's as valid a form of thruth as any other form of truth.

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:41.440
And, it's often a truth that can be usefully communicated to other people.

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:48.180
So, it's to the community's benefit you say what your experience has caught on, because

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:52.640
There isn't anyone who has exprience like you and one of the things that I've learned.

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:58.460
from being a clinical psychologist -but I would say more specifically from being a very careful listener-

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:03.840
is that if you listen to people AND they can talk to you, they tell you the most amazing things.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:08.180
You know, in my typical week, I prepare my work.

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:15.980
Even today, you know, I was speaking to four different people. They're supporters of mine (online).

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.840
And I meet with some of them.

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:25.820
And, I had four conversations that were just absolutely profound, you know. And it's so interesting, because

00:23:26.120 --> 00:23:29.960
if people are allowed / encouraged to express themselves

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:33.380
And that's something that you can do by listening to someone.

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:40.020
They open up and they're / it's as interesting as a [Fyodor] Dostoyevsky novel

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:42.640
People's actual lives are extraordinarily interesting.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:50.120
And it's important that they can speak freely about that, because then, they can tell you all the things they know that you don't know.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.680
and you get to know them, and that's such a good deal.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:55.660
So / so

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:02.700
I'm going to tell you some things that I believe to be true.

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:06.800
and, it will constitute the backbone of this talk.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.300
So the first thing is that life is suffering.

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:15.060
And then I would say that, this is an attempt to

00:24:15.740 --> 00:24:22.020
This is my attempt to reconcile the existence of multiple transcendant virtues.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:27.600
Transcendant virtues are the lights that guide you through your life, essentially.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:39.840
The principles by which it's necessary to live if you're going to have a life that's meaningful and profound and beneficial

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:46.440
There are multiple transendent values and it isn't clear how they relate to one another.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:51.760
This is my attempt to understand how they relate.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:57.940
So, I started with the truth that I believed to be supreme, which is that life is suffering.

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:05.820
And then, that love is the desire to see unnecessary suffering ameliorated.

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:10.080
And that truth is the hand-maiden of love.

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:15.840
And that dialogue is the pathway to truth.

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:25.360
and that humility is recognition of personal insufficiency.

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.040
and the willingness to learn

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:34.680
and that to learn is to die voluntarily and be born again

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:36.840
in great ways and small

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:41.600
so speech must be untrammeled

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:45.240
so that dialogue can take place

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:50.080
so that we can all humbly learn

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.160
so that truth can serve love

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:57.040
so that suffering can be ameliorated

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:02.760
so that we can all stumble forward, so to speak

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:04.760
to the kingdom of God

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:09.160
So I'll try to unpack that.

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:15.640
Well, I think the idea that life is suffering is

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:17.920
comes as no surprise to anyone

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:22.920
In my estimations, there's two reasons for that

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:27.520
One seems to have to do with the structure of being itself.

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:29.880
and by that, I mean

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:38.680
There seems to be something about limitation that is required for existence.

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:42.820
and it's a difficult notion to make clear, but I can tell you a little story about

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:50.800
What I thought when my son was young, when he was, maybe three(3) years old.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:55.000
I thought he was a remarkable creature.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.480
I thought the same about my daughter.

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:01.480
There is something remarkable about little kids, you know.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:10.160
They're little beacons of enthousiasm and wonder, I suppose.

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:13.800
although, it takes great effort to care for them

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:17.920
They repay you partly by their enthousiasm

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:23.760
and partly by the fact that they look at the world as if it's a new thing

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:26.180
and that allows you to do the same thing

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:28.320
when you're around them

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:30.760
so, they refresh the world for you

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:34.000
and that's a great thing

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:36.960
now, of course, if you're a parent

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:43.440
one of the things that's frightening about your children is that they're vulnerable

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:51.040
That's part of the burden of responsibility that's associated with being a parent

00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:54.880
but then, you might think of that vulnerability as a

00:27:55.760 --> 00:27:58.920
Well, as a liability, I suppose that's one way of thinking about it.

00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:03.960
But then I thought, well, that's an interesting way of looking at it, that it's only a liability

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:06.720
Because, maybe you would like your child not to be vulnerable, but

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:09.320
but then I thought that through

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:14.280
in a practical manner, I suppose, I thought well

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:17.960
'caus I always worried you know, he would go out in the street, a car would run over him

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.520
or he'd go into the playground and some bully would  knock him over, a dog would bite him

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:22.640
or something like that, you know

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:27.840
and, of course, little kids are always bumping themselves and falling down stairs and that kind of thing

00:28:29.840 --> 00:28:36.080
I thought: "well, what would you have to do to have a little being that didn't have those problems?"

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:44.120
well, you could give him a super-human intelligence, that would be a start

00:28:44.120 --> 00:28:47.240
he could be twenty(20)-feet-tall instead of three(3)-feet-tall

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:50.040
and he could be encased in titanium, and

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:54.440
He could be made invulnerable

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:58.960
or near-invulnerable, but the problem with that is you'd have to replace

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:02.920
what he was, piece by piece, until there was nothing left of him

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:07.940
and that was partly when I realized

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:12.160
because realizations like this, of course, unfold over time

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:14.560
when you deeply care for someone

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:17.360
you don't care for them despite their vulnerability

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:22.360
you care for them with the knowledge that their vulnerability is an intrinsic part of them

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:27.680
You care for the remarkable fact of their weakness and specificity

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:31.400
Just as much as you care for them for, whatever

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:34.800
abilities they bring to bear, whatever capacities they have

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:42.280
And so, to love someone is to simultaneously accept their vulnerability as a valid part of their being

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:47.280
and that's a necessary thing to do in life, because life is suffering

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:55.480
but without it, without the vulnerability that brings suffering, then, there doesn't seem to be any possiblity of

00:29:56.160 --> 00:30:04.600
individual existence, and so, to me, it seems that, in order for us to exist as individuals in some fundamentally real sense

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:08.600
we have to be limited in precisely the manner that we are limited

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.640
Being, in some sense, requires that limitation and that vulnerability

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:17.400
There's an old idea, I don't remember where I read it, it was many years ago

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.760
I think it was from Jewish commentary on the Torah

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:23.640
it was like a Zen Kōan, almost

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:25.000
The question was:

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:31.240
What does a being with the classical attributes of God    -Omniscience, Omnipotence and Omnipresence-

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:33.520
What does a being like that lack?

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:38.680
It's a very interesting question, because, of course, the obvious is, well, "nothing!"

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:44.440
but that's the wrong answer. The right answer is that being like that lacks limitation

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:50.880
The commentator was putting that argument forward as a justification for the creation of Man

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:54.640
And I thought that was a spectacularly brilliant insight

00:30:55.360 --> 00:31:02.560
that there's something that "something that lacks nothing" ... lacks, and that's lack itself

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:08.080
I read something about Kierkegaard, something written by Kierkegaard where he said the same thing: he said that

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:13.880
he knew himself as someone who was never going to be able to make life easy for people in the way that

00:31:13.880 --> 00:31:18.320
great inventors of the late 19th century had made things increasingly easy for people

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:25.040
His task was going to be to determine how it was that he could make life more difficult for people

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:30.000
He knew he would be thanked as one of the benefactors of the age for doing precisely that.

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:36.600
Kierkegaard attempted to, I suppose, lay a heavy moral burden on his readers

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:44.580
And he knew that was perhaps not something that people would appreciate immediately

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:48.680
but perhaps, on reflection, they would find that was exactly what they wanted

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:52.360
One of the things that's been very remarkable to me

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:56.400
-As I've been speaking to people recently- is that

00:31:56.920 --> 00:32:00.200
because I think that I'm doing something similar

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:07.000
I'm trying to lay a burden on people and, by all appearances, I'm trying to lay a burden on men

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:11.640
At least, that's who's responding to it. I mean, if you look at this audience

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:13.960
It must be 95% men

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:17.240
It's very very interesting to me, but

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:22.480
but there is something about responsability

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:25.680
that's associated with the transcendence of suffering

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:30.320
and that's absolutely necessary for life to proceed properly

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:39.400
So, life is suffering because we are vulnerable and it's also suffering, I would say because we are evil

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:42.560
And, I've tried to define evil

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:47.000
The tragic part of life is the fact that

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:51.560
our vulnerability is built in and we

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:56.120
and we have psychological problems and we have physical problems and we all die

00:32:56.680 --> 00:32:59.420
That's a part of the structure of existence

00:32:59.420 --> 00:33:01.400
it's the same structure that brings

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:06.560
about tornadoes and hurricanes and earthquakes and natural catastrophes of all types

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:10.120
it's the impartial brutality of reality

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:14.240
and that contributes greatly to human suffering

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:18.680
Although it may, as I said, also be a precondition for being itself

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:22.800
but then, there is the other kind of suffering that we bring upon ourselves

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:27.880
I would categorize that under the heading of malevolence

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:35.600
And malevolence is the desire to bring / to increase suffering in some sense for the sake of suffering itself: it's like an art form

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:39.240
I think that's the best way to conceptualize it: it's something aesthetic

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:46.320
It's the same aesthetic that drove the Nazis to put that terrible joke above the gates of Auschwitz which was

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.240
That work would make you free

00:33:48.240 --> 00:33:53.240
Of course, the freedom that was being offered in Nazi camps was death

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:57.480
That was a great / terrible joke

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:01.800
It's a very malevolent spirit that would dare to joke in that manner

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:06.480
but that's a spirit that we all have to contend with because there's part of each of us that

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:08.800
-because of the suffering that life entails-

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:14.320
is bitter about existence and would wish, in some sense, to see it eradicated

00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:20.040
That's something I learned from reading 
Goethe's great play, Faust,

00:34:20.180 --> 00:34:22.860
He has a character in there, Mephistopheles, who's

00:34:23.180 --> 00:34:26.820
who's sort of the intellectual version of Satan, I suppose.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:32.680
And Goethe has Mephistopheles attempt to explain the reason for his existence

00:34:32.680 --> 00:34:35.060
or, at least, the reason for his mode of being

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:40.400
Mephistopheles basically says -and Goethe has him say this twice-

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:46.500
Because existence is characterised by such dreadful suffering, it would be better if it never existed at all.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:49.580
and that's was he's bringing / working to bring about

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:55.280
That struck me in a very deep manner, because

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:58.080
I had recognized by that time that

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:02.660
Life was difficult enough so that is was

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:09.160
challenging to walk through it without becoming embittered and resentful, let's say

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:15.080
and then to become arrogant because of that and then to become a judge of existence because of that

00:35:15.080 --> 00:35:17.300
If you ever wonder, and of course you do

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:23.140
what is going through the minds of people who do such things and shoot up elementary schools full of children

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:29.340
knowing the things that I just described is very useful in developping some understanding

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.460
because, the people who do that are resentful, bitter and they've brooded upon it for, well not hours, but

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:38.420
weeks and months and years

00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:45.640
and they conclude that existence itself is evil and it should be taken revenge upon

00:35:45.740 --> 00:35:53.520
The way that you take revenge upon existence most precisely is to find the most innocent people and punish them the most dreadfully

00:35:53.880 --> 00:36:00.120
and that's the motive behind shooting elementary school kids in a playground, let's say

00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:07.200
that's a very dark place to go, but that's a place that people are perfectly capable of going

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:11.520
the terrible thing is that we have the motivation to go there

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:15.460
and you can make a rational case for going there

00:36:15.460 --> 00:36:18.020
but that does not make it the proper place to go

00:36:18.020 --> 00:36:20.940
and that brings me to the second point, which is

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:25.120
that love is the desire to see unnecessary suffering ameliorated

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:32.060
"love" is a very difficult word to use because, in some sense, it's being mouthed to death

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:36.780
you know, it's such a commonplace and if you're attempting to effect virtue

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:40.040
then, the first thing you do is speak about love

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:45.220
and it's very difficult to speak about love without feeling, I would say, self-contempt really

00:36:45.280 --> 00:36:48.320
because, it is a word that's misused so badly

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:53.660
but I'd like to offer a technical definition of it and then put it into relationship

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:57.760
into proper relationship with what I already discussed

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:07.200
if hatred is the desire to see being eliminated because of it's catastrophe

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:11.120
then love is the desire to see being elevated

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:14.980
maybe even BECAUSE of that suffering, and to say that

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:19.080
the proper orientation in life is to work diligently

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:25.440
for the alleviation of unnecessary suffering and to make that the core element of your being

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:29.080
your actual aim, because you need an aim, everyone needs an aim

00:37:29.560 --> 00:37:38.140
we're creatures / we're evolved to aim. We're hunters, for that matter, and we need a target to aim at

00:37:38.140 --> 00:37:42.000
we're not ever oriented properly without an aim, then you might say, well

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:47.980
why not choose an aim that is everything that you could possibly want it to be?

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:49.640
and I would say that

00:37:49.940 --> 00:37:53.260
that IS what you want to do because, first of all

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:58.960
being miserable and vulnerable and subject to catastrophe and decay

00:37:59.060 --> 00:38:05.180
It's easy to sink into self-contempt and misery 
(self-conscious misery)

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:08.360
and I think the only way out is to

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:14.520
voluntarily shoulder a burden that the shouldering of

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:19.260
grants you some respect for your own existence and so then you think, well

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:27.140
the way forward confidently is to find the biggest load that you can possibly lift, and then lift it

00:38:27.140 --> 00:38:32.020
and then walk forward with it because then, you can see that there's something to you

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:35.740
that transcends your vulnerability

00:38:36.020 --> 00:38:41.200
and ennobles the world and works against the very

00:38:41.860 --> 00:38:47.880
terrible problems of both suffering and malevolence that might drive you to despair to begin with

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:56.380
and so, when I'm working with my clinical clients and I think when I'm speaking to people in general when I'm properly oriented

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:03.340
This is the development of thought that was originally laid out in part by Carl Rogers

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:06.920
although he was deeply influenced by his Christian background

00:39:07.820 --> 00:39:10.180
he is a very famous psychologist

00:39:10.180 --> 00:39:15.440
He said that the way to treat people in therapy is with unconditional positive regard

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:22.320
and I would say that that's a noble formulation but it's erroneous because

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:26.280
that isn't exactly, in my estimation, what you should do with people

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:33.240
And I don't think it's what I do with people. What I do with people is to try to find the part of them that is striving towards the light

00:39:33.560 --> 00:39:38.300
and that would like being itself to be elevated and ennobled and suffering ameliorated

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:40.800
especially the unnecessary suffering

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:45.140
and then form an alliance with that, and communicate with that, and encourage that

00:39:45.140 --> 00:39:47.720
which is what I think you do to people that you love

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:51.480
and you can't do that if there's bitterness in your heart because

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:55.180
that's actually the part that you wanna destroy, if you're bitter

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:58.480
and as the story of Ken and Abel tells us

00:39:59.060 --> 00:40:04.360
in a terribly brief period of time, with terrible ?power?

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:12.640
you replace contempt for being and resentment and hatred for being with love

00:40:12.740 --> 00:40:18.460
And that's the desire to see the good flourish in you and in others

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:27.300
and I don't mean the good in that smarmy and contemptible manner that is often presented, I mean

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:35.060
the good as the culmination of brilliant awareness, courage and strength that it really is

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:43.260
and that you would wish to bring forward in everyone 
-to encourage in everyone-  
if you were oriented properly in the world

00:40:43.580 --> 00:40:46.660
and to seek that kind of orientation is to

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:50.880
round yourself in love instead of mephistophelian hatred

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:57.240
you have nothing better to do than that

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:00.520
you might as well do the best that you have to do, because

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:04.260
what do you have to lose? You're going to lose everything anyways

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:09.040
so, in the process of preparing to lose everything

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:14.520
you might as well risk everything you have doing the best thing that you can possibly conceive of

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:19.660
and there's no loss in that for you: there's nothing but gain

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:23.140
there's nothing but gain for everyone around you if you do that

00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:27.660
so why would you not do that?! except for bitterness and resentment

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:31.880
and perhaps a lack of confidence

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:36.820
perhaps because you haven't been encouraged properly, and that's too bad, because

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:41.660
that's a lot different than being empowered, by the way

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:46.280
to be encouraged, it's a much better word

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:52.180
so again, i wrote that truth is the hand-maiden of love

00:41:52.180 --> 00:42:04.800
and what I meant by that 
-because I've always wondered-
(knowing that Truth and Love are perhaps the cardinal virtues)

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:08.320
what is the proper way of conceptualizing their relationship?

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:12.260
and it seems to me that Truth has to be oriented towards something

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:18.000
it needs a context : there's no such thing as
-not in the way that I'm thinking of truth-

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:24.400
there's no such thing as context-free truth:
- not in the moral domain
-not in the domain of action

00:42:25.080 --> 00:42:29.380
and that's the / I'm talking about Truth in action

00:42:30.740 --> 00:42:34.260
Truth is the hand-maiden of Love, I suppose, because

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:38.740
having oriented yourself to the highest good that you can conceive of

00:42:40.020 --> 00:42:43.140
like Geppetto wishing on a star in Pinocchio

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:49.640
then you speak Truth without orientation in mind

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:55.920
having the faith, I suppose, that because Being is good,

00:42:56.880 --> 00:43:00.900
articulating its nature as clearly as possible

00:43:01.340 --> 00:43:06.120
is the best way of continuing to encourage that good to spring forward

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:13.180
Otherwise, you're presuming 
-if you use deceit and falsehood- that

00:43:13.480 --> 00:43:20.700
being is essentially corrupt, because otherwise you wouldn't have to falsify it in order to operate in the world

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:25.940
So you could say well / you could make the courageous assumption

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:28.140
that being is good

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:32.120
despite, I suppose, some evidence to the contrary

00:43:32.120 --> 00:43:36.160
and then take the great risk of speaking truth in relationship to that

00:43:36.460 --> 00:43:45.180
under the assumption that whatever speaking truth produces -if it's oriented properly- is Good

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:51.200
you have to make decisions like that in your life

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:57.400
I could give you a simple example, I suppose

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:01.840
a while back, I was speaking at McMaster University

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:04.800
and there were a lot of protesters there

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:12.280
that made me nervous because you never know when someone is going to do something unforgivably stupid

00:44:12.460 --> 00:44:20.880
and, so far, I've been fortunate that 
-in the controversies that I've been brought in- 
that no one has done that

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:26.580
they pushed the edges of that, but they haven't done it

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:28.700
and, truly, thank God for that

00:44:29.220 --> 00:44:31.180
but, having said that,

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:35.440
I wasn't upset by the presence of the protesters, because

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:39.240
I couldn't tell if their presence was a good thing or a bad thing

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:46.620
it could be a good thing, it could be a bad thing, it was a consequence of what I've been speaking of, and that's what happened

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:52.400
and so, I thought there was no reason not to let that play itself out, because

00:44:52.960 --> 00:45:00.700
the end of the story had not yet been reached and the meaning of the events could not yet be specified

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:06.240
You know, sometimes, in your family, if you tell someone the truth / and I don't mean ...

00:45:06.680 --> 00:45:08.300
I hope I've coined this

00:45:08.300 --> 00:45:11.740
I don't mean black truths
-you know, there's white lies-

00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:18.260
a white lie occurs to preserve a higher moral virtue

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:20.740
you contaminate lower moral virtue

00:45:20.740 --> 00:45:24.740
you know, perhaps you don't tell a child dying of cancer that they're going to die

00:45:24.740 --> 00:45:28.360
I don't know, because it depends very much on the particulars of the situation

00:45:28.360 --> 00:45:31.660
You can understand that you have mixed motives in a situation like that

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:39.520
you can also speak black truths : black truths is when you say something that's nominally true, but you're using it as a weapon for another purpose

00:45:39.660 --> 00:45:47.120
and that's not a truth, that's really a more profoundly evil lie that any other one that you could possibly manage, because

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:54.480
you're using the truth in a way that corrupts it
-corrupts the truth itself!-
and that's really reprehensible

00:45:54.940 --> 00:46:02.440
but, barring the use of the black truth, you know, if, within your family, you say what you mean, you say what you think

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:06.440
that can often cause tremendous upheaval, because

00:46:07.300 --> 00:46:11.720
well, because many relationships are cobbled together by

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:18.920
various alliances of willful blindness and things left unspoken, that's a very bad long-term strategy

00:46:20.560 --> 00:46:25.980
I've seen people who are embroiled in the death throes of a relationship

00:46:26.560 --> 00:46:30.940
that perhaps accrue a hundred-thousand lies over it's course

00:46:30.940 --> 00:46:34.640
and became so unstable because of that that there was no hope for it

00:46:34.840 --> 00:46:39.540
Each of those lies was a forestalled opportunity

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:42.860
to address something difficult with thruth that was forgone

00:46:43.140 --> 00:46:50.060
and all that does is make those unsolved problems accumulate and multiply

00:46:50.060 --> 00:46:54.880
and they eventually take form and attack generally when you least expect it

00:46:55.180 --> 00:47:02.020
so, if you speak the truth in your family, cautiously and carefully

00:47:02.420 --> 00:47:05.180
and knowing that you could be wrong

00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:09.100
you will cause upheaval and conflict

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:16.060
but it's possible that that's the least amount of upheaval and conflict

00:47:16.480 --> 00:47:20.160
that could exist to make things right

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:24.480
I believe that is the case

00:47:24.620 --> 00:47:31.700
one of the things that I tell people who are too agreeable and, let's say, who don't like to cause conflict ...

00:47:32.080 --> 00:47:36.740
-and I actually don't like to cause conflict, because I'm more agreeable than I should be-

00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:45.100
... is that the ethical requirement to tell the truth trumps any desire to avoid conflict

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:52.040
and it's partly because you only forestall the conflict and magnify it

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:59.800
there's no escaping it and it's better to engage in it directly when the necessity first arises

00:47:59.840 --> 00:48:02.020
than to forestall it

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:08.820
so, for people who are only too willing to make peace at the expense of themselves, let's say

00:48:09.560 --> 00:48:13.840
I try to encourage them to generate conflict

00:48:14.100 --> 00:48:15.740
by telling the truth

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.220
and, I would say inevitably

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:25.000
that has nothing but beneficial medium-to-long-term consequences in their life

00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:29.780
even though it exposes them to more conflict in the short-term

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:33.120
it also alleviates their resentment, because

00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:36.140
if someone has made you resentful

00:48:36.660 --> 00:48:40.420
or if you become resentful because of someone's actions

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:44.380
then there are only two real reasons why:
- one is that ...

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:49.160
You should pull up your socks and quit complaining

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:55.600
quit whining because you're being required to show responsibility,

00:48:56.800 --> 00:49:04.060
or you're being oppressed and tyrannized by someone who doesn't know where the proper limits are and in the former case,

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:11.680
you should get your act together, and in the latter case, you should stand up and stop that person from encroaching upon you.

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:21.480
So you speak truth in your family let's say perhaps even to yourself

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:26.820
at the risk of conflict, often severe conflict but the ultimate goal is to bring peace,

00:49:26.820 --> 00:49:33.460
it's paradoxical because we often don't think of peace as something that's reached through conflict

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:36.460
but that is precisely, for example, why we value free speech

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:40.900
because the wisdom of humanity and the wisest people that we know,

00:49:40.900 --> 00:49:47.580
that would include those people who were intelligent enough to found this country on the principles that is founded on

00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:59.840
knew that whatever conflict free speech might produce pale in comparison to the conflict that was generated by tyranny and repression of the exchange of opinion

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:05.060
and so it's never a matter of picking a safe path because there's no such thing as a safe path

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:10.920
it's only a matter of picking a path that produces the least catastrophe possible

00:50:11.780 --> 00:50:18.740
It's an inviolable principle I would say, and also the fundamental principal of Western civilization,

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:26.580
that speech freely exchange is the best pathway to peace and redemption that we have identified.

00:50:27.660 --> 00:50:33.640
And so it should remain untrammeled  under all circumstances possible,

00:50:33.780 --> 00:50:39.620
subject to very infrequent restrains of the sort that are already encapsulated in law.

00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:45.580
Such that, for example you cannot incite someone to criminal activity.

00:50:50.080 --> 00:50:50.800
Humility,

00:50:52.460 --> 00:50:55.360
sorry, dialogue, is the pathway to truth

00:50:56.340 --> 00:51:02.240
Well, understand that, you have to understand why we think

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:10.800
and the reason we think is for the same reason that animals, including us, act.

00:51:10.800 --> 00:51:16.820
And the reason that animals act is so that they can survive in the world, they do think so that they can survive in the world.

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:26.400
But the problem with doing things in the world, the problem with acting in the world is that you can act in the manner that

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:31.960
causes the cessation of your being, you can act in a manner that gets you killed

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:36.660
you can act in a manner that makes you sick, you can make a mistake and you pay for that.

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:46.840
That's the danger of acting, and so human beings, have replaced mere acting with thinking, and what you do when you think, is you produce variants of yourself,

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:55.900
and you walk them through an imaginary time and place, and you know what happens to those imaginary variants as they act out

00:51:55.900 --> 00:52:02.060
those particular patterns of behavior and you know which ones get into trouble and which ones succeed

00:52:02.060 --> 00:52:08.160
and you let the ones that get into trouble die and you let the ones succeed grip hold of your behavior and act them out in the world.

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:15.400
It was a brilliant innovation, you might say, on the part of our ancestors

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:25.040
to grow cognitive resources, sophisticated enough to produce variants of ourselves, in a fictional space,

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:27.060
so that our thoughts could die instead of us.

00:52:27.220 --> 00:52:31.940
And so that's why we think - so that our thoughts could die instead of us.

00:52:33.440 --> 00:52:41.440
And then, the reason that we speak is to learn from other people's thoughts, but more than that,

00:52:41.780 --> 00:52:47.520
it's that thinking individually is a very limited process

00:52:47.520 --> 00:52:50.380
because you're ignorant, which means you don't know everything

00:52:50.860 --> 00:52:54.140
and not only that, you're biased in many many ways

00:52:54.140 --> 00:52:58.640
you're biased by what you know and what you don't know, you're biased by what you assume,

00:52:58.640 --> 00:53:05.400
you're biased by how you deceive yourself, you're biased by your temperament and your locale and your position in history,

00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:09.340
all of these things make you a very limited observer of the world.

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:15.220
And so think as you might, you can never think sufficiently.

00:53:15.440 --> 00:53:18.820
And so what we do because of that is talk.

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:26.220
And so, if I talk, and I'm speaking truthfully, which means I'm telling you how things appear to be to me,

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:35.120
that does not mean that I possess the truth, that means I'm saying as articulate in my manner as I possibly can how things appear to me,

00:53:35.120 --> 00:53:37.520
then you can tell me how things appear to you,

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:43.240
and I can listen to you and maybe you can tell me some things about how things appear that I don't already know,

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:50.340
and that's such a good deal because the purpose of thinking is to let our thoughts die instead of us,

00:53:50.700 --> 00:53:57.700
and if you can kill one of my thoughts because it's stupid, then I don't have to act it out and die and so that's like a really good deal

00:53:58.240 --> 00:54:06.160
So, the reason that we engage in dialogue is that we can take the thoughts that protect us from our own mortality

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:11.960
and improve them, and perhaps improve them erratically and there's just no loss in that,

00:54:12.020 --> 00:54:18.220
and so what that means in some sense is that you can view everyone who disagrees with you as a source of potential wisdom

00:54:18.220 --> 00:54:25.040
because if you talk to someone who agrees with you, all you're going to find out is what you already know and you already know that

00:54:25.160 --> 00:54:27.480
and if you talk to someone who doesn't agree with you,

00:54:27.700 --> 00:54:33.220
maybe one of the ten things they say or maybe one of a hundred things or God only knows, you know,

00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:38.660
maybe they're as wise as you in their perverse way and you can discuss something with them

00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:48.160
and they'll tell you something you've never really thought of before, and that'll just make some potentially miserable event in your like that much less likely

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:50.660
and how good for you can that possibly be?

00:54:50.740 --> 00:54:57.740
I do believe that I've learned that, I've talked to a great variety of people, and I don't care if people agree with me,

00:54:57.740 --> 00:55:01.000
in fact, generally speaking, I find it more interesting if they don't,

00:55:01.420 --> 00:55:08.140
and I don't try to convince people because I don't believe I have any right to determine their destiny

00:55:08.140 --> 00:55:12.440
and I don't want to determine their destiny because I don't know what's best for them,

00:55:13.160 --> 00:55:21.200
and so I do listen to all sorts of strange and unlikely people and they tell me things that I don't know all the time

00:55:21.200 --> 00:55:24.080
and that is just a spectacularly good deal.

00:55:24.620 --> 00:55:35.760
And I decided a long time ago that one of the fundamental choices you make in life is whether you're in love with what you know or in love with what you don't know,

00:55:35.760 --> 00:55:40.900
and I would say there's a lot more of what you don't know than there is of what you know

00:55:40.900 --> 00:55:46.880
and so it would be better to be in love with what you don't know because you don't know so much

00:55:46.880 --> 00:55:52.560
that if you can make friends with what you don't know, then you're going to be continually enlightened.

00:55:53.060 --> 00:56:00.060
And to be enlightened is to be engaged in the process of being enlightened, not to be enlightened in any final sense

00:56:00.060 --> 00:56:01.900
because that's just not possible.

00:56:02.660 --> 00:56:08.820
So it's to act in a manner that continually enlightens you and a huge part of that is humility,

00:56:08.820 --> 00:56:12.340
and that's another word that's very difficult to use, I would say,

00:56:12.340 --> 00:56:17.020
like love, because it's another word that's been mouthed to death in some sense

00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:22.120
and it sounds like kind of cringing cowardliness.

00:56:24.120 --> 00:56:26.800
That it has that connotation, but that's not what it means

00:56:26.800 --> 00:56:31.880
what it means is that you know that there isn't enough to you,

00:56:31.880 --> 00:56:36.140
and you can tell that there isn't enough to you because your life isn't everything it could be.

00:56:36.140 --> 00:56:37.760
That's the evidence, right?

00:56:37.760 --> 00:56:43.400
If your life is everything you could imagine it to be, then perhaps you don't need humility

00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:49.360
but since your life isn't everything you could imagine it to be, then you're deeply wrong about a lot of things,

00:56:49.640 --> 00:56:56.180
and so since you're wrong, then you should be in around for the opportunity to correct those errors

00:56:56.180 --> 00:56:57.460
because why wouldn't you?

00:56:58.040 --> 00:57:05.640
Except to protect your territory and that territory by definition, given the existence of your own suffering, is insufficient

00:57:05.780 --> 00:57:09.100
so why are you clinging to it so desperately?

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:12.860
It doesn't provide what you need or what you want

00:57:13.860 --> 00:57:20.860
and a transformation of it, and the expansion, and the re-figuring of it might be everything that you can possibly want

00:57:26.100 --> 00:57:33.100
and so then you start to envision yourself instead of, as the holder of a piece of territory, as the person who is attempting to expand their capacity,

00:57:33.660 --> 00:57:42.120
and to act within all sorts of territories, and exposure to more people and to more viewpoints, especially if those people are telling you the truth,

00:57:42.460 --> 00:57:45.740
which they will do if you actually listen to them,

00:57:46.300 --> 00:57:49.020
then you can have your cake and eat it too.

00:57:49.400 --> 00:57:50.940
That's a great deal.

00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:55.180
It's another way of reducing unnecessary suffering in the world by,

00:57:55.540 --> 00:58:06.720
certainly, reducing your suffering and perhaps, learning to be - how to be a greater use to people around you, which again, seems to be nothing but for the good.

00:58:10.260 --> 00:58:15.080
So humility is recognition of personal insufficiency and the willingness to learn,

00:58:15.080 --> 00:58:17.600
in order to overcome that insufficiency,

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:23.120
and then to learn is to die voluntarily and be born again, in great ways and small.

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:28.180
That's an archetypal idea at the basis of Western civilization,

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:33.200
is the idea that the logos, which is the capacity for truthful speech,

00:58:33.460 --> 00:58:38.320
is somehow associated with the act of death and rebirth

00:58:39.380 --> 00:58:42.980
and there's multiple ways of interpreting that idea,

00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:47.260
and because I'm a psychologist, I tend to interpret it psychologically

00:58:47.260 --> 00:58:51.600
and I don't claim that the psychological interpretation exhausts the concept,

00:58:51.860 --> 00:58:59.620
because I don't believe it does, but you see, you are a certain way and some of that way is an error

00:58:59.720 --> 00:59:06.720
and then when you learn that an error has taken place, you have to let go of the conceptions that produced that error,

00:59:06.760 --> 00:59:09.240
and that could be very, very painful.

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:14.100
For example, you may have entered into a relationship that was pathological,

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:19.820
and you know it's pathological but you're hooked into it and you're emotions are hooked into it and you've configured you life around it

00:59:19.820 --> 00:59:25.460
and then in order to move forward, you have to let go of that, you have to admit to all the errors you made

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:30.880
including the time that was wasted and you have to allow a large part of yourself to die,

00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:38.380
let's say, and you experienced that in grief and misery and sorrow and anxiety and sleepless nights and all of that,

00:59:38.540 --> 00:59:41.180
but it's better than dying completely,

00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:47.720
and what that indicates is that every time you learn something, large or small,

00:59:48.120 --> 00:59:56.500
the part of you that was in error prior to obtaining that bit of information has to deteriorate and disappear.

00:59:56.500 --> 00:59:58.540
There's pain associated with that.

00:59:58.540 --> 01:00:02.340
But then there's the possibility for regrowth and transformation,

01:00:04.960 --> 01:00:11.960
and one of the most fundamental decisions that human beings made in their lives is whether or not they're going to identify with what they are or with what they could be,

01:00:11.960 --> 01:00:17.140
and if they identify with what they could be, then they have to continually let what they are die

01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:20.100
so that what they could be can come forward.

01:00:20.880 --> 01:00:26.000
And so to learn is to die voluntarily and be born again in great ways and small.

01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:38.980
So then I wrote that speech must be untrammeled, so that dialogue can take place.

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:48.600
I think of the discussions that I've had with my wife and my kids, but primarily with my wife,

01:00:48.600 --> 01:00:50.280
who's a very honest person,

01:00:50.840 --> 01:00:55.600
in fact, I don't - I've been married for 28 years

01:00:57.060 --> 01:00:59.700
I've known her for 45 years

01:00:59.700 --> 01:01:03.360
and I'm 55 - that's a very long time, we've been friends a very long time

01:01:03.860 --> 01:01:07.420
and when she first came to being with me,

01:01:07.820 --> 01:01:13.420
30 years ago, I was learning some of the things that I'm talking to you about tonight,

01:01:13.420 --> 01:01:16.260
and I told her that if we were going to be together,

01:01:19.620 --> 01:01:23.600
that she would have to tell the truth because if she didn't tell the truth and I wouldn't know who she was,

01:01:23.600 --> 01:01:29.420
and I wouldn't know where we were, I wouldn't be able to solve any problems and neither would she,

01:01:29.420 --> 01:01:34.380
cause we'd be living a fictional relationship with one another, and that struck me as a very bad idea.

01:01:34.880 --> 01:01:36.880
And so, she took that to heart,

01:01:37.540 --> 01:01:38.640
I would say,

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:41.720
and I would also say it's like the more honest person than me.

01:01:43.200 --> 01:01:53.860
And I'm not claiming to be spectacularly morally virtuous in any particular manner, and I'm quite aware of my multitude of faults,

01:01:54.480 --> 01:02:00.640
but the fact that she has gripped so tightly onto the truth means that if I ever have a problem,

01:02:00.720 --> 01:02:03.840
that I can talk to her and I know what she thinks.

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:06.280
