But the idea is that that's your best bet.

01:50:56.600 --> 01:50:59.000
All things considered it's your best bet

01:50:59.000 --> 01:51:04.460
to face it forthrightly and I believe that, I partly believe that, because I'm a clinical psychologist

01:51:04.900 --> 01:51:13.140
and one of the things that clinical psychologists know is that if you help people confront the things that they avoid,

01:51:14.040 --> 01:51:16.120
voluntarily, they get better.

01:51:16.120 --> 01:51:22.620
And that's a fundamental truth, I think that's the fundamental truth of human advancement and learning

01:51:23.000 --> 01:51:32.620
and it's possible that we are Irish elk, and their fundamental approach to the world is deeply flawed and if we pursue it whole-heartedly we'll end up extinct.

01:51:33.000 --> 01:51:42.260
It is possible that that's the case but if that's the case there's nothing I can do about it, I'm part of the human race and I'm going to act out its destiny the best that I can manage

01:51:42.800 --> 01:51:47.740
and I'm not willing to reconsider things at that level,

01:51:47.740 --> 01:51:52.060
I think that that's because I've thought them through sufficiently for me,

01:51:52.240 --> 01:52:03.480
and because I've also determined in the course of my own life that, (and from watching the effects of what I have been talking about on other people), what I'm doing is serving the good.

01:52:03.720 --> 01:52:05.640
At least as well as I can do it.

01:52:05.920 --> 01:52:17.500
And what I've learned has been very helpful to me personally, it's been very helpful to my family, I know it's been helpful for my students because they tell me that, and people write me all the time

01:52:18.500 --> 01:52:24.920
say that they found great utility in what I'm communicating.

01:52:24.960 --> 01:52:37.720
And so that's enough evidence for me right now that I'm on a path that's sufficient...that sufficiently justifies me.

01:52:41.060 --> 01:52:53.880
Just like to say we have about 20 minutes until nine o'clock, we're probably going to take five to ten minutes of more questions and then we have about ten minutes for people to talk amongst themselves and make their way out, so, up to ten minutes more.

01:52:56.600 --> 01:53:00.920
I'd just like to thank everybody who helped organize this event,

01:53:01.220 --> 01:53:14.360
I've been watching yourself on YouTube for a while now, and I really felt you've helped me and I'm sure a lot of other people in this room, I really appreciate it.

01:53:15.120 --> 01:53:16.560
Hey it's a pleasure :)

01:53:17.520 --> 01:53:30.160
So I was just wondering, based on history and whatnot that you go into discuss, like the Gulag Archipelago and whatnot,

01:53:30.160 --> 01:53:32.360
what kind of role do you think the government should play in society?

01:53:46.500 --> 01:53:49.560
I think that it depends on the circumstances

01:53:51.380 --> 01:53:54.900
and so, I don't think that that's the right question.

01:53:55.640 --> 01:54:05.740
I think the right question is: how should the government constitute itself so that it can respond flexibly  the demands of the situation?

01:54:06.640 --> 01:54:18.100
I believe that there's utility in the conservative viewpoint, and I believe that there's utility in the liberal viewpoint, but that it depends on the specifics of the situation.

01:54:18.440 --> 01:54:23.240
And that there is also error, and even catastrophic error on both sides.

01:54:23.700 --> 01:54:28.760
So that the government needs to be constituted, I would say, much like it is in the West

01:54:29.780 --> 01:54:36.780
where sovereignty is instantiated into people for better or worse, so that people can learn from their errors.

01:54:37.220 --> 01:54:45.320
And that we progress by engaging in dialogue across people of different temperamental types.

01:54:46.060 --> 01:54:56.220
Because sometimes the boarders are too open, and they need to be tightened; and sometimes the boarders are too closed, and they need to be loosened.

01:54:56.220 --> 01:55:01.740
If the boarders between everything become completely closed, then everything dies, right?

01:55:01.740 --> 01:55:03.440
It's deprived of air of water.

01:55:03.440 --> 01:55:09.700
And everything dies. There's endless mythological representation of that being part of the reason that Egypt, for example,

01:55:09.700 --> 01:55:15.280
and the Mosaic story is a Kingdom of sand and stone, because sand and stone lack water,

01:55:15.500 --> 01:55:19.640
and Moses who's someone who leads his people out of tyranny,

01:55:19.640 --> 01:55:21.140
is a master of water.

01:55:21.140 --> 01:55:25.100
And the message in that story is that sometimes the Kingdom turns to stone,

01:55:25.120 --> 01:55:26.400
and it's a tyranny.

01:55:26.560 --> 01:55:30.580
And new revivification needs to be integrated

01:55:30.580 --> 01:55:33.920
the doors have to open and new information has to flow in.

01:55:33.980 --> 01:55:37.820
But then there are times when, like the story for example,

01:55:39.260 --> 01:55:47.460
there are times when there's too much influx of information, that's a flood, that's one way of reading the flood story,

01:55:47.460 --> 01:55:51.220
is that there's just too much pouring in, and everyone drowns

01:55:51.380 --> 01:55:56.580
and what we have to always ask ourselves is: are we tending the guard properly?

01:55:57.300 --> 01:56:03.940
It's not drying out from lack of water, it's not drowning because there's too much moisture coming in,

01:56:03.940 --> 01:56:10.320
and the only way we can determine that is by paying attention and communicating, and that's why free speech is such a fundamental necessity.

01:56:10.420 --> 01:56:15.860
So it's not precisely what the government can do, because it has to do many things,

01:56:15.860 --> 01:56:20.280
but, because it's the society of people and people have to do many things

01:56:20.280 --> 01:56:23.960
but how the government goes about in making its decisions

01:56:24.220 --> 01:56:36.940
And then, at least what the government has to do is protect those, what we call rare and precious processes, that enable it to continue to be a responsive and alive entity.

01:56:36.940 --> 01:56:40.260
And I think we have done a remarkably good job at that in the West,

01:56:40.260 --> 01:56:43.060
and I think that the US is a great example of that.

01:56:43.260 --> 01:56:52.440
For all its flaws, everything is flawed, but the proper analysis is flawed compared to what?

01:56:52.500 --> 01:57:00.840
And the answer isn't to the hypothetical utopia of your imagination, the answer is to other similar systems that have actually existed in time and space.

01:57:00.840 --> 01:57:05.160
And by that measure, I would say that the West is doing spectacularly well.

01:57:05.480 --> 01:57:07.440
And I don't think that we should sink it.

01:57:08.220 --> 01:57:12.100
Because of...because we're ignorant about its value.

01:57:22.960 --> 01:57:30.780
About the Postmodern philosophers that you've mentioned that's having particularly threatening and destructive ideas

01:57:30.780 --> 01:57:36.860
that cause people to question and re-silent our culture, like you've mentioned Foucault,

01:57:36.960 --> 01:57:51.360
who has mentioned Egypt as his inspiration, so I wonder how might somebody like Foucault or someone like me who can think along with somebody like Foucault get each other along(?)

01:57:52.200 --> 01:57:57.500
Well, I've read a reasonable amount of Foucault

01:57:57.960 --> 01:58:04.960
and I would say that among the Postmodernists he's rather remarkable in the fact that you can actually understand what he say.

01:58:05.700 --> 01:58:13.880
I don't find what he wrote particularly surprising, like the idea that mental illness is in part a social construct,

01:58:14.000 --> 01:58:20.600
is...if you're a reasonably educated clinician, that's more than self-evident

01:58:21.160 --> 01:58:28.160
in that virtually every complex activity that human beings engage in is culturally determined to a large degree.

01:58:28.880 --> 01:58:31.420
It's Derrida that I have the most problem with.

01:58:32.120 --> 01:58:46.780
And I also know that he put his finger on a very complex problem, which is that the central Postmodernist claim is that every situation allows for a potentially infinite number of interpretations,

01:58:46.860 --> 01:58:47.820
which is true.

01:58:47.820 --> 01:58:54.180
And therefore it's difficult to determine which interpretation should be held up as paramount.

01:58:55.280 --> 01:59:02.280
Now, that's true. In fact, the artificial intelligence researchers ran into that when they were trying to design systems that can perceive the world

01:59:02.280 --> 01:59:07.460
because the original idea was that perceiving the world is easy, because it's just made out of things,

01:59:07.460 --> 01:59:13.160
and that learning how to manipulate those things and act upon them would be the complicated problem

01:59:13.160 --> 01:59:19.160
but it actually turned about that perception was the complicated problem because there's an indefinite number of ways  to perceive things.

01:59:20.000 --> 01:59:30.760
Now, the reason that Postmodernists were wrong was that there aren't indefinite number of interpretations of the world that don't kill you.

01:59:30.900 --> 01:59:32.500
Or make others kill you.

01:59:32.500 --> 01:59:39.180
There's a very constraint number of interpretations that allow you to continue to exist and equally importantly,

01:59:39.180 --> 01:59:42.580
to allow you to continue to exist in the presence of other people.

01:59:42.700 --> 01:59:52.260
So although, there are many interpretations that you can ferret out of the Shakespeare kit, your job is to ferret out one that helps you live properly.

01:59:52.260 --> 01:59:56.260
And that's hypothetically the goal of the guides to great literature

01:59:56.260 --> 02:00:00.020
And so I think the Postmodernists radically overplayed their hand,

02:00:00.020 --> 02:00:02.420
and that's particularly true for Derrida,

02:00:02.700 --> 02:00:15.820
and I think the people who follow Derrida are in many cases doing so because it's hell of a lot easier than actually doing the difficult job of extracting the useful tools from the literature of the world,

02:00:15.820 --> 02:00:18.820
which is what you do when you read, you extract out useful tools

02:00:18.980 --> 02:00:23.860
With regards to Nietzsche, Nietzsche said something about everything.

02:00:23.860 --> 02:00:30.060
And so every philosopher of the modern age, one way or another, is deeply influenced by Nietzsche.

02:00:30.320 --> 02:00:37.320
And the problem with Nietzsche is that he was a figure prolific beyond comprehension, virtually, I mean,

02:00:41.780 --> 02:00:48.780
recently I did, I think it was a 45 minute video on one paragraph of Nietzsche's "Beyond Good and Evil" and you know, I've barely started to scrape the surface of it,

02:00:48.780 --> 02:00:50.660
and of course he has multiple books.

02:00:51.480 --> 02:00:58.360
So Nietzsche is a very strange character, and he said that he philosophies with a hammer and he meant it.

02:00:58.480 --> 02:01:02.780
And his critque of Christianity was devastating, I would say,

02:01:03.160 --> 02:01:16.240
but I would also say, and this is a rather shocking comparison, that the role Nietzsche played with regard to Christianity was the same role a maggot can play at a wound,

02:01:16.240 --> 02:01:20.860
which is that it can devour nothing but diseased flesh and leave what's healthy behind

02:01:20.860 --> 02:01:25.320
and I think that there is a reading of Nietzsche that allows for precisely that.

02:01:25.660 --> 02:01:28.300
You know, when he said that God was dead,

02:01:28.300 --> 02:01:33.460
he also said that that would be a catastrophe, the lights with which we could not even imagine

02:01:33.460 --> 02:01:38.600
and society itself would oscillate back and forth murderously for centuries as a consequence.

02:01:38.600 --> 02:01:40.120
So wasn't triumphant.

02:01:40.460 --> 02:01:48.440
And he strove to find out what an alternative to that might be and he formulated the idea of over-man (ubermensch), essentially, right?

02:01:48.520 --> 02:01:57.560
The superman who was capable of creating his own values, and I think that Nietzsche made a mistake there, and I think it was a mistake that was rectified by Carl Jung,

02:01:57.800 --> 02:02:04.800
because Jung knew that...I think Jung was really the first person, although Nietzsche has intimations of this,

02:02:04.860 --> 02:02:11.860
because Nietzsche does say that our drives philosophies, right? which is very biologically oriented observation.

02:02:12.000 --> 02:02:24.260
But what Jung noted was that well we don't create our own values because the values are implicit within us, but we can rediscover them, and that's partly why he was so interested is analysis in mythology and the unconscious.

02:02:24.720 --> 02:02:35.380
And so, I think that Postmodernists took the part of Nietzsche that's only destructive to its final conclusion, and that's actually what's being played out now.

02:02:36.640 --> 02:02:48.780
Derrida's critique of modern civilization is Phallogocentric, right? male dominated, centered around the idea of the logos - he regarded that as nothing but a tool of oppression and marginalization.

02:02:49.100 --> 02:02:57.820
And there's some truth in that because whenever you produce a value system, you also exclude things from the value system as not valuable, obviously

02:02:58.000 --> 02:03:12.940
but the problem I think that the Postmodernists won't address is that the alternative to creating value systems and dealing with the exlusion that comes with that is to drown in Nihilistic chaos,

02:03:13.220 --> 02:03:20.760
and worse, while you're drowning in Nihilistic chaos, you're going to call out forces of totalitarian order.

02:03:21.060 --> 02:03:22.660
And so it's no solution.

02:03:22.660 --> 02:03:29.220
And what I see Postmodernism doing to students, generally speaking, is demoralize them completely.

02:03:29.220 --> 02:03:33.380
You know, they come to university looking to catalyze their identity,

02:03:33.620 --> 02:03:45.340
often clinging to last remaining shred of their culture that's just what's keeping them afloat and all that Postmodernist professors do is take those remaining fragments that are allowing them to stay above the water and

02:03:45.340 --> 02:03:48.260
tell them that they're illusory and take them away.

02:03:48.260 --> 02:03:50.160
It's like well now you're free.

02:03:50.160 --> 02:03:55.120
It's like I've dropped you in the middle of the ocean, you can swim in any direction you want,

02:03:55.460 --> 02:03:56.340
you're free.

02:03:57.020 --> 02:04:03.100
And I think that that's rooted in large part, even in hatred of youth. I really believe that.

02:04:03.100 --> 02:04:06.120
Because why would you cripple people if you didn't hate them?

02:04:06.120 --> 02:04:11.760
And so there's hatred of humanity lurking at the bottom of the worst access of the idealogues in general.

02:04:11.760 --> 02:04:18.640
You know, you hear this from environmentalists sometime, I've heard this many times: the planet will be better off if there were no people on it.

02:04:18.640 --> 02:04:22.700
It's like, well let's keep you away from the third nuclear weapons, shall we?

02:04:31.040 --> 02:04:36.200
So it's not easy to come up with canonical interpretation of Nietzsche, there's no doubt about that,

02:04:36.300 --> 02:04:37.900
but, again, I would say,

02:04:38.900 --> 02:04:47.300
I thought for a long time, from a psychological perspective, I tried to understand, whether it was Nietzsche's philosophy that drove him insane.

02:04:47.300 --> 02:04:54.100
But I think the evidence for that is non-existent, I think he did suffer from a degenerative brain disease of great severity

02:04:54.100 --> 02:05:02.680
and I actually think that his fundamental philosophy which I've read - I've read everything he wrote because there's been new books published, but I read his great works in chronological order

02:05:02.760 --> 02:05:08.960
I think his fundamental philosophy is affirmative, not negative.

02:05:08.960 --> 02:05:14.700
But he's a critic, you know? and he wanted to find something that he can stand on and that's what critics do

02:05:14.700 --> 02:05:18.600
if you're a real critic, you're not looking to destroy, you're looking for something that you can stand on

02:05:18.600 --> 02:05:25.120
And Nietzsche was certainly very useful to me and I know he was also extraordinarily useful to Carl Jung, who's a real student of Nietzsche,

02:05:25.440 --> 02:05:32.700
and he was really devoting himself to solving the problem that Nietzsche posed, which is what do we do in the aftermath of the death of God?

02:05:33.020 --> 02:05:40.000
And Jung's answer was: we rescue our father from the depth, and that's the right answer; that's what universities should be doing with young people,

02:05:40.000 --> 02:05:42.020
it's like your dead father's in the library

02:05:42.360 --> 02:05:49.360
go out there and fought(?) and ferret him out, right? and unite with him, and become the thing that keeps chaos at bay.

02:05:59.380 --> 02:06:06.380
On that note, this concludes our talk with Jordan Peterson, please join me in thanking him again for joining us!

